---
title: Kannadiga Podi
time: 1 hour
servings: 50? 100? 200? Who knows?
tags:
    - podi
---

Heat @gingelly oil{1%tsp} in a #pan{}. Roast the @asafoetida{5} until it swells on all sides and becomes soft enough to crush with a spoon.

Add the @dried Kashmiri chillies{1}(loosely packed cup) and roast until crisp and fragrant, without letting them blacken. Transfer everything to a plate and leave it to cool.

In the same pan, heat one more teaspoon of gingelly oil. Roast the @urad dal{1%cup} and @chana dal{2%tbsp} until the urad dal turns a deep rust-brown. Set aside to cool.

Now roast the @coconut{2½%cups} and @curry leaves{1%cup} over medium heat, stirring continuously. The coconut can go from golden to burnt very quickly, so stay close. Once evenly brown, set it aside and let everything cool completely.

Grind the @hing{} and @dried Kashmiri chillies{} first, until you have a bright-red powder. Transfer it to a large container.

Next, grind the roasted urad dal, chana dal, @salt{}, and a little of the chilli powder. Add this to the same container.

Finally, add the coconut, @curry leaves{}, @dry tamarind{1}(1-inch piece, cut small), @jaggery powder{3%tbsp} and a spoonful of the ground lentils to the mixie. The lentil powder absorbs some of the oil released by the coconut and helps the mixture remain dry.

Pulse only once or twice; we want a coarse, grainy texture, not a fine powder.

Mix all three ground mixtures thoroughly. Taste and adjust the salt or jaggery powder before storing in a clean, dry jar.

> Best eaten with rice and dal or curd rice. It goes great with dosas and idlis as well.